WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.033 --> 00:00:01.701
This morning, rushing floodwaters,

00:00:01.701 --> 00:00:02.769
shutting down major

00:00:02.769 --> 00:00:03.870
roads, leaving no

00:00:03.870 --> 00:00:04.704
way in or out

00:00:04.704 --> 00:00:06.873
across much of Southern California.

00:00:06.873 --> 00:00:08.842
Hillary is the first tropical storm

00:00:08.842 --> 00:00:10.744
in more than 25 years to move

00:00:10.744 --> 00:00:11.478
through the state.

00:00:11.478 --> 00:00:13.580
Breaking all kinds of records.

00:00:13.947 --> 00:00:14.647
Los Angeles

00:00:14.647 --> 00:00:16.416
recorded its rainy summer day

00:00:16.416 --> 00:00:18.818
and at least 150 years.

00:00:19.319 --> 00:00:20.053
Palm Springs

00:00:20.053 --> 00:00:21.187
got a full year's

00:00:21.187 --> 00:00:23.690
worth of rain in 24 hours.

00:00:23.723 --> 00:00:26.092
Its 911 system is still down.

00:00:26.092 --> 00:00:27.260
There were mudslides

00:00:27.260 --> 00:00:28.762
in the San Bernardino Mountains.

00:00:28.762 --> 00:00:29.829
Look at that.

00:00:29.829 --> 00:00:30.864
And now

00:00:30.864 --> 00:00:33.566
the storm that did this is moving north.

00:00:34.334 --> 00:00:35.368
CNN's Stephanie Elam

00:00:35.368 --> 00:00:36.903
joins us from Cathedral City,

00:00:36.903 --> 00:00:38.671
just east of Palm Springs.

00:00:38.671 --> 00:00:41.775
Stephanie, what a muddy, icky mess

00:00:41.775 --> 00:00:43.410
behind you.

00:00:45.145 --> 00:00:47.013
I have a nice pun for you, John.

00:00:47.013 --> 00:00:48.515
It's called the Meeting of the Mud.

00:00:48.515 --> 00:00:49.049
Take a look.

00:00:49.049 --> 00:00:49.482
You can see the

00:00:49.482 --> 00:00:50.950
two different kinds of mud.

00:00:50.950 --> 00:00:51.518
Believe it or not,

00:00:51.518 --> 00:00:52.585
I'm standing on a road,

00:00:52.585 --> 00:00:53.253
a major road

00:00:53.253 --> 00:00:55.655
in and out of the Palm Springs area here.

00:00:55.955 --> 00:00:57.190
This is what's going on.

00:00:57.190 --> 00:00:58.525
They're starting to clean it up now

00:00:58.525 --> 00:01:00.460
that the sun has come and come out.

00:01:00.460 --> 00:01:01.194
But look,

00:01:01.194 --> 00:01:02.429
there's two cars over here

00:01:02.429 --> 00:01:03.897
that have been stuck since we were here.

00:01:03.897 --> 00:01:04.964
In the darkness.

00:01:04.964 --> 00:01:07.067
And now you can see how high that mud

00:01:07.067 --> 00:01:08.835
is up on their wheels.

00:01:08.835 --> 00:01:09.869
And then over to the right,

00:01:09.869 --> 00:01:11.771
there's this little Volkswagen,

00:01:11.771 --> 00:01:12.939
some very respectful,

00:01:12.939 --> 00:01:14.040
thoughtful individual

00:01:14.040 --> 00:01:15.341
just went flying through the mud

00:01:15.341 --> 00:01:17.110
on the wrong side of the road

00:01:17.110 --> 00:01:18.645
to get onto the side

00:01:18.645 --> 00:01:19.779
and completely splashed

00:01:19.779 --> 00:01:21.181
our photojournalist Mike Love,

00:01:21.181 --> 00:01:23.450
who is now completely covered in mud.

00:01:23.450 --> 00:01:25.485
But that's how far she got.

00:01:25.518 --> 00:01:27.187
That's where she's ended up here.

00:01:27.187 --> 00:01:29.289
These are the reasons why they're saying

00:01:29.522 --> 00:01:30.423
schools are closed.

00:01:30.423 --> 00:01:31.091
These are the reasons

00:01:31.091 --> 00:01:31.658
why they're saying

00:01:31.658 --> 00:01:32.425
people will not be able

00:01:32.425 --> 00:01:33.259
to get in and around

00:01:33.259 --> 00:01:34.327
and into Palm Springs

00:01:34.327 --> 00:01:35.862
because out in the distance there,

00:01:35.862 --> 00:01:37.530
I just walked all the way up there.

00:01:37.530 --> 00:01:39.666
That would be towards Interstate ten.

00:01:39.666 --> 00:01:41.334
There are Mack Trucks.

00:01:41.334 --> 00:01:42.635
There are cars

00:01:42.635 --> 00:01:43.403
twisted off

00:01:43.403 --> 00:01:45.338
to the side of the road up there.

00:01:45.338 --> 00:01:47.574
It's completely blocked right now

00:01:47.707 --> 00:01:50.243
with vehicles and mud and running water

00:01:50.410 --> 00:01:52.479
that is still finding its way

00:01:52.479 --> 00:01:53.446
off the road.

00:01:53.446 --> 00:01:55.215
And down off into the wash.

00:01:55.215 --> 00:01:57.450
All of their washes are full.

00:01:57.450 --> 00:01:58.852
When you look at this, now that

00:01:58.852 --> 00:01:59.652
the sun has come up,

00:02:00.887 --> 00:02:02.355
it's almost unbelievable

00:02:02.355 --> 00:02:04.257
how much of a mess is out here.

00:02:04.257 --> 00:02:05.525
And when you put your foot

00:02:05.525 --> 00:02:07.127
into some of this mud,

00:02:07.127 --> 00:02:08.361
they just came through and plowed here.

00:02:08.361 --> 00:02:10.497
So it's not as sticky.

00:02:10.497 --> 00:02:11.131
But, you know,

00:02:11.131 --> 00:02:11.731
a couple of times

00:02:11.731 --> 00:02:13.133
I thought I was going to lose my boot

00:02:13.133 --> 00:02:14.934
because it's so sticky.

00:02:14.934 --> 00:02:16.903
And that is what's trapping

00:02:16.903 --> 00:02:18.171
these cars out here.

00:02:18.171 --> 00:02:18.771
And think about it,

00:02:18.771 --> 00:02:20.874
they were doing this in the darkness.

00:02:20.874 --> 00:02:22.242
And you can see up there

00:02:22.242 --> 00:02:24.277
what the mess it has left behind.

00:02:24.277 --> 00:02:26.212
I sent in some video to show you

00:02:26.212 --> 00:02:28.381
of just how bad it is out here.

00:02:28.381 --> 00:02:29.415
It's unbelievable

00:02:29.415 --> 00:02:31.985
now that the sun is up here, John.

00:02:31.985 --> 00:02:33.052
It really is gross.

00:02:33.052 --> 00:02:34.487
And we saw that front end loader

00:02:34.487 --> 00:02:36.156
working behind you.

00:02:36.156 --> 00:02:37.290
I've seen snow plows.

00:02:37.290 --> 00:02:38.258
I'm not sure I've ever seen

00:02:38.258 --> 00:02:40.326
mud plows before.

00:02:40.426 --> 00:02:42.462
That was like mud plowing. Step

00:02:44.797 --> 00:02:47.400
Also mud plowing in August.

00:02:47.567 --> 00:02:48.468
Just think about that.

00:02:48.468 --> 00:02:49.736
This was the rainiest

00:02:49.736 --> 00:02:51.571
they have ever had in one day

00:02:51.571 --> 00:02:52.805
in Palm Springs.

00:02:52.805 --> 00:02:54.674
For 100 years of records.

00:02:54.674 --> 00:02:57.043
And it's very odd to have that much rain

00:02:57.410 --> 00:02:58.912
in Southern California,

00:02:58.912 --> 00:03:00.446
let alone in August.

00:03:00.446 --> 00:03:00.847
All right,

00:03:00.847 --> 00:03:02.515
Stephanie Elam, keep your boots on.

00:03:02.515 --> 00:03:03.149
My advice.

00:03:03.149 --> 00:03:06.019
Duct tape works wonders if you need it.

00:03:06.019 --> 00:03:08.221
Thank you very much for your reporting.

00:03:09.556 --> 00:03:10.456
You would know.

00:03:10.456 --> 00:03:11.457
Let's get the latest now

00:03:11.457 --> 00:03:13.026
from Cathedral City, actually, right

00:03:13.026 --> 00:03:14.894
where Stephanie Elam is reporting.

00:03:14.894 --> 00:03:16.129
Cathedral City's fire chief,

00:03:16.129 --> 00:03:18.131
Michael Contreras is joining us now.

00:03:18.131 --> 00:03:19.365
Chief, thanks so much for coming in.

00:03:19.365 --> 00:03:21.301
Stephanie Elam from where she is,

00:03:21.301 --> 00:03:22.068
as the sun is coming

00:03:22.068 --> 00:03:23.169
up, she's seeing

00:03:23.169 --> 00:03:24.537
just the muddy mess

00:03:24.537 --> 00:03:25.471
that is all over

00:03:25.471 --> 00:03:26.673
some of the streets there.

00:03:26.673 --> 00:03:27.640
What are your crews?

00:03:27.640 --> 00:03:28.575
What are your crews seeing

00:03:28.575 --> 00:03:29.209
out there right now?

00:03:31.544 --> 00:03:32.845
Yeah, we're right in the

00:03:32.845 --> 00:03:34.280
middle of that right now.

00:03:34.280 --> 00:03:36.516
We're in all areas around that,

00:03:36.516 --> 00:03:38.351
that muddy area

00:03:38.351 --> 00:03:40.486
and that intersection right there

00:03:40.486 --> 00:03:41.721
that had a combination

00:03:41.721 --> 00:03:43.656
of what we call a debris flow.

00:03:43.656 --> 00:03:45.725
So a large amount of mud and sand

00:03:45.992 --> 00:03:47.227
moving into that area,

00:03:47.227 --> 00:03:50.863
which is what trapped the cars there.

00:03:50.863 --> 00:03:52.532
We were able to

00:03:52.532 --> 00:03:54.801
extricate and rescue seven people there.

00:03:55.134 --> 00:03:57.003
So now we're going house to house

00:03:57.003 --> 00:03:57.770
and making sure

00:03:57.770 --> 00:03:58.671
that the

00:03:58.671 --> 00:04:01.507
people that want to get out can get out.

00:04:02.242 --> 00:04:05.311
We've just called in a dozer or bulldozer

00:04:05.945 --> 00:04:07.313
and they're going to go and clear

00:04:07.313 --> 00:04:08.448
those neighborhoods

00:04:08.448 --> 00:04:10.383
so that those people can get out

00:04:10.383 --> 00:04:10.950
because right now

00:04:10.950 --> 00:04:12.252
they're trapped for all intents

00:04:12.252 --> 00:04:13.353
and purposes.

00:04:13.353 --> 00:04:14.220
Talk to me about that

00:04:14.220 --> 00:04:15.855
because the mayor of Palm Springs,

00:04:15.855 --> 00:04:16.489
which is,

00:04:16.489 --> 00:04:17.223
you know, near nearest

00:04:17.223 --> 00:04:19.492
by saying that they don't even have

00:04:20.126 --> 00:04:20.727
access in

00:04:20.727 --> 00:04:21.928
and out of Palm Springs,

00:04:21.928 --> 00:04:24.130
is completely blocked right now.

00:04:24.330 --> 00:04:24.931
What?

00:04:24.931 --> 00:04:27.333
How would you describe the situation

00:04:27.333 --> 00:04:28.268
as the water

00:04:28.268 --> 00:04:30.637
as the storm itself has moved past

00:04:30.937 --> 00:04:32.338
what is now left behind

00:04:32.338 --> 00:04:33.673
that you're seeing in Cathedral City?

00:04:35.875 --> 00:04:38.011
Yeah, there's a lot of destruction.

00:04:38.011 --> 00:04:40.513
Right. And Mother Nature

00:04:40.513 --> 00:04:42.482
clearly put her mark on us

00:04:42.482 --> 00:04:44.684
over the last 48 hours.

00:04:44.684 --> 00:04:45.051
And that's

00:04:45.051 --> 00:04:46.919
what you're seeing with that mud.

00:04:46.919 --> 00:04:49.188
And it's a combination of mud and sand

00:04:49.489 --> 00:04:51.724
that makes it really hard to get through.

00:04:51.724 --> 00:04:52.959
The water, for the most part,

00:04:52.959 --> 00:04:54.661
has dissipated.

00:04:54.661 --> 00:04:55.862
It's drained off.

00:04:55.862 --> 00:04:58.298
It's moved out into the drains.

00:04:58.898 --> 00:05:00.266
But what we're really dealing with

00:05:00.266 --> 00:05:01.934
is that excess mud

00:05:01.934 --> 00:05:04.170
that is a lot like quicksand.

00:05:04.704 --> 00:05:06.039
It's real heavy

00:05:06.039 --> 00:05:07.307
and takes a lot of effort.

00:05:07.307 --> 00:05:09.509
So if you're elderly

00:05:09.509 --> 00:05:11.611
or you know you're a young person,

00:05:11.611 --> 00:05:13.112
it's really hard to get through that.

00:05:13.112 --> 00:05:14.147
And so that's

00:05:14.147 --> 00:05:15.682
that's our main focus right

00:05:15.682 --> 00:05:17.984
now is to clear the roads.

00:05:17.984 --> 00:05:18.551
Right now,

00:05:18.551 --> 00:05:19.952
we have the I-10 freeway,

00:05:19.952 --> 00:05:21.954
which is the main thoroughfare

00:05:21.954 --> 00:05:23.656
through both Palm Springs

00:05:23.656 --> 00:05:25.858
and Cathedral City.

00:05:25.858 --> 00:05:27.894
Our off ramps are on.

00:05:27.894 --> 00:05:29.729
Both theirs and ours are limited

00:05:29.729 --> 00:05:30.930
because of the mud.

00:05:30.930 --> 00:05:32.532
So it's really about clearing the roads

00:05:32.532 --> 00:05:33.900
and getting access

00:05:33.900 --> 00:05:35.935
and then assisting Caltrans

00:05:36.202 --> 00:05:37.203
with getting the

00:05:37.203 --> 00:05:39.272
the I-10 open in both directions.

00:05:39.572 --> 00:05:40.039
Absolutely.

00:05:40.039 --> 00:05:40.907
And Chief,

00:05:40.907 --> 00:05:44.110
we we had seen reports that the 911

00:05:44.210 --> 00:05:46.746
phone lines were down in your city.

00:05:47.146 --> 00:05:48.981
Is that still the case? What's happening

00:05:51.250 --> 00:05:51.651
No.

00:05:51.651 --> 00:05:52.952
You know, what happened

00:05:52.952 --> 00:05:56.422
there is there was no 911 calls missed

00:05:57.090 --> 00:05:57.390
there.

00:05:57.390 --> 00:05:59.625
There was with the carrier.

00:05:59.859 --> 00:06:01.794
They had an issue with flooding.

00:06:01.794 --> 00:06:04.597
But, you know, we prepare for this

00:06:04.597 --> 00:06:06.866
all the time, both fire and police.

00:06:06.866 --> 00:06:09.302
And we were able to transition.

00:06:09.302 --> 00:06:10.470
And we sent that out

00:06:10.470 --> 00:06:11.671
and we moved those lines

00:06:11.671 --> 00:06:14.107
over to what we call the business lines.

00:06:14.107 --> 00:06:15.041
It's a different number,

00:06:15.041 --> 00:06:16.042
but it still comes through

00:06:16.042 --> 00:06:17.076
the 9 million system.

00:06:17.076 --> 00:06:19.645
So, again, we were prepared for it.

00:06:20.546 --> 00:06:22.715
But we do routinely.

00:06:23.416 --> 00:06:23.850
Yeah.

00:06:23.850 --> 00:06:25.852
I mean, that's good to hear you.

00:06:25.852 --> 00:06:27.253
You had mentioned that you think

00:06:27.253 --> 00:06:28.020
folks are

00:06:28.020 --> 00:06:28.988
essentially trapped

00:06:28.988 --> 00:06:31.557
because of this mud, sand mixture, mess

00:06:31.858 --> 00:06:32.792
that's left behind.

00:06:32.792 --> 00:06:33.960
I mean, what's your best advice

00:06:33.960 --> 00:06:35.395
for people right now?

00:06:35.395 --> 00:06:36.462
You've got crews out.

00:06:36.462 --> 00:06:37.864
People are going to need some

00:06:37.864 --> 00:06:39.532
need rescued today.

00:06:39.532 --> 00:06:40.867
But what's your best advice for folks

00:06:43.069 --> 00:06:43.302
yeah.

00:06:43.302 --> 00:06:44.070
Right now, if

00:06:44.070 --> 00:06:45.138
they don't need anything,

00:06:45.138 --> 00:06:46.406
if they don't have.

00:06:46.539 --> 00:06:47.607
Well, let me back up.

00:06:47.607 --> 00:06:49.675
It's the same thing that we told people

00:06:49.675 --> 00:06:51.144
coming into the storm, right?

00:06:51.144 --> 00:06:52.945
If you don't need to be on the road

00:06:52.945 --> 00:06:54.180
don't leave your house,

00:06:54.180 --> 00:06:55.782
get the supplies you need

00:06:55.782 --> 00:06:57.750
and stay warm in your house.

00:06:57.750 --> 00:06:58.851
That's kind of the same thing

00:06:58.851 --> 00:06:59.619
we're saying now.

00:06:59.619 --> 00:07:01.954
If you don't need to get out, let's

00:07:01.988 --> 00:07:02.889
give us some time.

00:07:02.889 --> 00:07:03.990
We'll get

00:07:03.990 --> 00:07:06.526
the tractors, we'll get the manpower

00:07:07.293 --> 00:07:08.761
and we'll clear the streets.

00:07:08.761 --> 00:07:09.829
And then that way

00:07:09.829 --> 00:07:12.465
it'll be in a lot more organized fashion.

00:07:12.765 --> 00:07:14.233
What our biggest concern is

00:07:14.233 --> 00:07:16.302
that our people are still out there,

00:07:17.437 --> 00:07:19.439
both from all city departments,

00:07:19.439 --> 00:07:20.740
from public works

00:07:20.740 --> 00:07:21.641
to facilities

00:07:21.641 --> 00:07:22.708
the building department

00:07:22.708 --> 00:07:25.445
that may have to read tags and buildings.

00:07:25.711 --> 00:07:27.647
And when we're out there doing our job

00:07:27.647 --> 00:07:29.982
and people are in a hurry,

00:07:29.982 --> 00:07:31.117
it puts us at risk.

00:07:31.117 --> 00:07:33.453
So you know, if they can just stay home

00:07:33.453 --> 00:07:34.921
and give us a little time

00:07:34.921 --> 00:07:36.889
and then we'll go from there.

00:07:36.889 --> 00:07:37.190
Chief,

00:07:37.190 --> 00:07:38.057
thanks so much for jumping

00:07:38.057 --> 00:07:39.292
on this morning.

00:07:39.292 --> 00:07:41.093
Really appreciate your time.

00:07:41.093 --> 00:07:42.995
This is brand new video out of San Diego.

00:07:42.995 --> 00:07:43.830
The city

00:07:43.830 --> 00:07:45.998
facing such an inundation of water.

00:07:45.998 --> 00:07:47.700
It was shooting out of manholes

00:07:47.700 --> 00:07:48.701
at one point.

00:07:48.701 --> 00:07:50.336
CNN's Mike Blair is in San Diego

00:07:50.336 --> 00:07:51.671
with the latest

00:07:51.671 --> 00:07:53.573
Mike, there's a wild to see

00:07:53.573 --> 00:07:54.807
how much water was shooting

00:07:54.807 --> 00:07:55.708
out of the manholes there.

00:07:55.708 --> 00:07:56.709
But what have you been seeing

00:07:56.709 --> 00:07:57.810
and hearing this morning as the

00:07:57.810 --> 00:07:58.511
sun's coming up

00:08:01.380 --> 00:08:02.148
Yeah, you know, Kate,

00:08:02.148 --> 00:08:02.849
that picture,

00:08:02.849 --> 00:08:04.650
those images certainly are crazy.

00:08:04.650 --> 00:08:06.853
But I think that we are all feeling

00:08:06.853 --> 00:08:09.121
a sense of shared relief in San Diego

00:08:09.121 --> 00:08:10.523
and the coastal communities

00:08:10.523 --> 00:08:11.891
up and down the Pacific coast

00:08:11.891 --> 00:08:14.060
that we seem to have been spared

00:08:14.360 --> 00:08:16.462
from the brunt of the disaster,

00:08:16.462 --> 00:08:17.296
the brunt of the damage

00:08:17.296 --> 00:08:18.631
that is really belonging

00:08:18.631 --> 00:08:19.932
to the desert communities

00:08:19.932 --> 00:08:21.000
about 90 miles

00:08:21.000 --> 00:08:23.069
to our north and east Palm Springs,

00:08:23.069 --> 00:08:24.070
the Coachella Valley,

00:08:24.070 --> 00:08:25.571
even farther north towards

00:08:25.571 --> 00:08:27.306
Death Valley and the Nevada border.

00:08:27.306 --> 00:08:28.941
I think also what civic

00:08:28.941 --> 00:08:30.142
authorities are telling us,

00:08:30.142 --> 00:08:31.210
they are

00:08:31.344 --> 00:08:32.478
saying that they have

00:08:32.478 --> 00:08:33.412
that sense of relief

00:08:33.412 --> 00:08:35.147
because of the capricious whims

00:08:35.147 --> 00:08:36.949
of the track that this storm took

00:08:36.949 --> 00:08:39.252
and B, the sense of preparation

00:08:39.252 --> 00:08:40.486
that muscle memory

00:08:40.486 --> 00:08:42.088
from the atmospheric rivers

00:08:42.088 --> 00:08:43.756
that we had earlier

00:08:43.756 --> 00:08:44.390
this year,

00:08:44.390 --> 00:08:46.058
and that is due

00:08:46.058 --> 00:08:47.994
to all of the extra personnel.

00:08:47.994 --> 00:08:49.262
There were not one,

00:08:49.262 --> 00:08:50.663
but ten water

00:08:50.663 --> 00:08:51.364
rescue teams

00:08:51.364 --> 00:08:53.666
positioned up and down the San Diego

00:08:53.666 --> 00:08:54.433
River from Mission

00:08:54.433 --> 00:08:55.701
Bay, close to SeaWorld,

00:08:55.701 --> 00:08:56.936
all the way up to Interstate

00:08:56.936 --> 00:08:57.837
five, the five,

00:08:57.837 --> 00:08:59.739
making sure that everybody was okay

00:08:59.739 --> 00:09:02.508
rescuing nine people overnight Kate.

00:09:02.642 --> 00:09:05.611
So I think that just San Diego County,

00:09:05.611 --> 00:09:06.512
for example,

00:09:06.512 --> 00:09:09.448
getting a whopping 30,000 people

00:09:09.448 --> 00:09:10.816
to sign up for their emergency

00:09:10.816 --> 00:09:11.851
alert systems,

00:09:11.851 --> 00:09:12.618
texting people

00:09:12.618 --> 00:09:13.619
when something would go wrong,

00:09:13.619 --> 00:09:15.121
when roads would be flooded.

00:09:15.121 --> 00:09:16.856
There is a shared sense

00:09:16.856 --> 00:09:18.391
that this is the new normal.

00:09:18.391 --> 00:09:19.158
We've got to get used

00:09:19.158 --> 00:09:20.026
to this kind of weather.

00:09:20.026 --> 00:09:20.426
And people

00:09:20.426 --> 00:09:21.761
are certainly rising to the occasion

00:09:21.761 --> 00:09:24.664
here. Kate, Michaela in San Diego for us.

00:09:24.697 --> 00:09:25.364
Thank you, Mike.

